WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.340
Front-end end-to-end testing can be

00:02.360 --> 00:04.620
super flaky, but Speedscale's new

00:04.640 --> 00:06.940
Playwright automated testing fixes that.

00:07.700 --> 00:09.700
Normally, every time a test breaks,

00:09.920 --> 00:10.840
you have to figure out whether

00:10.860 --> 00:12.360
you broke the page, the API

00:12.380 --> 00:14.640
changed, or somebody reset the data

00:14.660 --> 00:16.060
in your shared test environment.

00:16.780 --> 00:18.600
So before you can investigate your

00:18.620 --> 00:20.620
change, you're investigating everything

00:20.640 --> 00:22.840
around the change, including its environment.

00:23.980 --> 00:25.760
Speedscale's browser journeys are

00:25.780 --> 00:27.620
unique because they run Playwright

00:27.640 --> 00:29.300
tests and then connect them to

00:29.320 --> 00:31.780
the API traffic behind the change.

00:32.040 --> 00:33.720
You can run a normal end-to-end

00:33.740 --> 00:35.220
test against your working frontend and

00:35.240 --> 00:37.840
backend, or you can record the

00:37.860 --> 00:40.280
API responses, turn the backend off,

00:40.300 --> 00:41.740
and run the same test again.

00:41.820 --> 00:43.160
You are no longer tied to

00:43.180 --> 00:44.500
a test environment, and you can

00:44.520 --> 00:46.240
run it locally or in CI.

00:46.620 --> 00:48.160
The browser still loads the real

00:48.180 --> 00:49.960
frontend, clicks the button, and checks

00:49.980 --> 00:50.820
what the user sees.

00:50.840 --> 00:52.980
Its backend requests are answered by

00:53.000 --> 00:54.660
controlled mocks built from that

00:54.680 --> 00:56.900
recording, orchestrated all as part of

00:56.920 --> 00:57.720
the same test.

00:58.660 --> 01:00.420
You can also run the real

01:00.440 --> 01:02.720
API and compare its responses with

01:02.740 --> 01:04.840
the recording, helping identify backend

01:04.860 --> 01:07.300
changes behind a failed browser step.

01:08.660 --> 01:11.240
The useful part is choosing what

01:11.260 --> 01:13.220
the test depends on, the whole

01:13.240 --> 01:15.120
deployed system or a known set

01:15.140 --> 01:16.760
of backend responses that let you

01:16.780 --> 01:19.400
test the frontend without a stable shared environment.

01:20.040 --> 01:21.700
The full walkthrough is in our

01:21.720 --> 01:23.760
blog, including the demo and commands

01:23.780 --> 01:24.640
to try it yourself.

01:24.780 --> 01:26.220
Just follow the link and have

01:26.240 --> 01:28.160
your AI set up your own Playwright tests.
